[{:seon.task/name "a-resource-name-stays-in-its-root"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by jolt/similar-finds-helpers-by-embedding (2026-10-23): in a worktree nested under the main tree (next/.worktrees/NAME), `seon.config-test/an-unknown-key-refuses-boot-naming-it` and `an-invalid-value-refuses-boot-naming-each-key` read the MAIN tree's resources/seon/config/default.edn, so a branch that adds config keys is red there and one that removes keys could be falsely green. Cause: Jolt's resource lookup over jolt.loader's roots, `resource-candidate` (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/java/io.ss, called from `resolve-resource`) builds root + \"/\" + name with no check that the normalized path stays under root, and aero's adaptive resolver tries ``clojure.java.io/resource`` before the including file's directory, so test/seon/config/unknown-key.edn's #include \"../../../resources/seon/config/default.edn\" climbs from the worktree's src/ root out to next/resources/. The JVM's resource lookup answers nil for a name that leaves its root. Reproduction, in a nested worktree: HOME=$PWD/target/home JOLT_PWD=$PWD target/jolt/jolt -A:test -e '(println (str (clojure.java.io/resource \"../../../resources/seon/config/default.edn\")))' prints a file: URL into the main tree; the JVM prints nil. Correct, in our Jolt fork on a jolt/<topic> branch: `resource-candidate` answers no candidate for a name whose normalized path leaves its root. Notes: side-session-notes config-include-leak.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.loader-test/clojure-java-io-resource-resources-seon-config-default}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-aot-cache-is-bounded-by-bytes-least-recently-used-first"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: Jolt's AOT cache (``JOLT_CACHE_DIR``, the main tree's ``target/cache/aot``, shared by every checkout: `docs/source.md#shared-build-caches`) keeps ``JOLT_CACHE_GENERATIONS`` (4, ``bin/env``) generations, one per runtime, pruned by last use (``aot-prune-generations!`` in ``reference-code/jolt/host/chez/loader.ss``); inside a generation nothing is ever removed but an artifact that failed to load (``aot-safe-load-or-recompile``'s recover). An artifact is ``<ns>[-dl]-<own>-<full>.so`` beside its captured ``.scm`` (``aot-publish-artifact!``): own is the source's bytes and classpath name (``aot-read-own-key``), full folds every direct dependency's contribution, the digest of the files its compile read and the data readers' digest (``aot-base-full``). A dependency that defines a macro, record, protocol or forwarded var (``aot-ct-relevant?``) contributes its whole namespace digest, transitively (``aot-dep-contribution``, ``aot-ns-digest``); only an inert one contributes its var names. So any edit to a relevant namespace publishes a new artifact for every namespace above it, under a new name, and the old one stays. Measured 2026-11-11 (read only, the shared cache): 27 GB (18 GB on 11-06), 30,845 artifacts, .so 14.5 GiB and .scm 12.5 GiB, sidecars 0.6 MiB. By generation: the old pin's (``g88c36ac0``) 15.4 GiB, 17,106 artifacts of 500 namespaces (1,039 source versions); the current pin's (``g122445b2-B6857F``) 7.8 GiB, 10,178 of 481 (918 versions), 9,258 of them written on 11-10 alone; a dev runtime's 3.1 GiB, 2,166 of 84 (332 versions); the other current-pin runtime 0.7 GiB. Per namespace: mean 21-34 artifacts in the three large generations (4.4 in the small one), max 262 (``my.root``); per source version (the same text under another full key) mean 6.5-16.5, max 219: it is many versions of each file's consumers, not many files. Top by bytes in the current generation: seon.render-test 612 MiB (61 artifacts, 24 versions), seon.agent-test 491 (79, 31), seon.ctx.wire-test 474 (87, 9), seon.render.ui 399 (133, 22), seon.root 317 (155, 18), seon.reconcile 110 (80 artifacts of 2 versions). 57 of Seon's 65 src namespaces with a ``.ct`` sidecar are relevant, so nearly any src edit re-keys every namespace that requires it. The newest artifact of each namespace is 0.07-0.28 GiB a generation: what one tree needs. Cause: a cache whose entries are keyed by content and never evicted grows with the number of distinct trees compiled under one runtime (each worktree edit, each merge), and the generation bound counts runtimes, not bytes. Upstream Jolt (``jolt-lang/jolt`` main) is the same: only generations are pruned. Jolt's build-unit cache in the same repository already has the bound this needs: ``JOLT_BUILD_CACHE_MB`` (default 2048), oldest mtime first (``bld-prune-build-cache!`` in ``host/chez/build.ss``), a hit moving the entry's mtime to now (``bld-cache-fetch!``), and the main tree's ``target/cache/build`` stands at 2.0 GB, at its bound. Correct, in our Jolt fork on a jolt/<topic> branch (Generic: an upstream candidate): the AOT cache takes the build cache's bound, not a second policy. A hit marks its artifact used (its .so's mtime set to now, one utimes); a process that published an artifact then prunes the whole cache root (every generation's v1) to a byte budget (``JOLT_CACHE_MB``, set by ``bin/env`` beside ``JOLT_CACHE_GENERATIONS``), least recently used first, never an artifact younger than ``aot-prune-grace-seconds``, the .so removed before its .scm (a reader sees a whole artifact or a miss; the own-key sidecars, 0.6 MiB in all, stay). A per-tree bound (one artifact per source and requires-keys reachable from the current tree) is not what this cache can know: it is shared by checkouts on different trees, and a hit on a live worktree's tree must survive another's build. Cost: n = artifacts in the cache; the bound is bytes, so at a 4 GiB budget and 0.80 MB an artifact (.so mean 473 KB, .scm 331 KB) n is about 5,000; a prune lists and stats about 10,000 files (measured: 23,447 files in 48.7 ms), only in a process that compiled (already over 100 ms); a hit costs one utimes, about 500 a process start. The cache would hold the budget, against 27 GB now. Fix: about 30 lines of Scheme in ``loader.ss`` (the build cache's prune shape) and one line in ``bin/env``; the wiki owes `docs/source.md#shared-build-caches` the bound and `docs/machine.md#disk-stays-bounded` its number in place of the 18 GB, and ``bin/env``'s comment its \"Seon's is 140 MB\" (a generation is 0.7-15 GiB). Proof to write: a gate in our Jolt fork, named jolt.loader-test/an-aot-cache-past-its-budget-drops-least-recently-used-first: under a small budget, three namespaces published, the oldest-used one's artifact removed after a fourth publishes, one hit in between keeps the one it hit, and every remaining artifact loads."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.loader-test/an-aot-cache-past-its-budget-drops-least-recently-used-first}}
